<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>ReliableOrders.Local</RootNamespace>
    <Description>
      The local development stack's own program. It provisions the emulated queues and tables, and
      then stands in for the Lambda event source mapping by reading batches off the queue, invoking
      the function through the runtime interface emulator, and acting on the response. Nothing here
      is deployed; see the Local Development Stack section of docs/testing-strategy.md.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Core for the two table schemas, so provisioning cannot create a shape the writer does not
      write. Function for the names of the environment variables it reads: the tables this program
      creates are the tables that function was told to use, and reading both from one set of
      constants means a rename fails this build rather than the first message of a demonstration.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\ReliableOrders.Core\ReliableOrders.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\ReliableOrders.Function\ReliableOrders.Function.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The queue naming and the redrive setting are copies of what the CDK deploys, and LocalStackParityTests
    is what holds the copies honest. It synthesises the development stack and compares the template
    against the constants here, which are internal because nothing outside this program has business
    calling them.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="ReliableOrders.CdkTests" />
    <!-- For LocalConfigurationTests, which covers the environment this program refuses to start on. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="ReliableOrders.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.DynamoDBv2" />
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.SQS" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
